+## TL;DR
+- `LangChain`, `LangGraph`, and `AutoGen` are the strongest starting points for agentic workflows and multi-step orchestration.
+- `Dify`, `Flowise`, `Langflow`, `Open WebUI`, and `AnythingLLM` are the most practical GitHub-first app layers for teams that want faster prototyping.
+- `Ollama`, `vLLM`, `llama.cpp`, `Text Generation Inference`, and `LocalAI` cover the main local and self-hosted model serving patterns.
+- `Langfuse`, `Phoenix`, `promptfoo`, `Ragas`, and `DeepEval` are the best-known open source options for tracing, testing, and LLM evaluation.
+- `Qdrant`, `Weaviate`, `Milvus`, `Chroma`, and `Haystack` remain core building blocks for retrieval, indexing, and RAG pipelines.
+
+## Why This List
+Open source matters for LLM engineering because teams need auditability, self-hosting, extensibility, and the ability to swap models or infra without being locked into one vendor. This list focuses on the best AI tools for LLM development 2026 that are available as public GitHub repositories, so you can inspect the code, verify the license, and build on top of real projects instead of marketing pages.
+
+The scope is intentionally strict: every tool below has a `github.com` repository URL, and proprietary SaaS-only products are excluded. That makes this list more useful for developers comparing agent frameworks, local runtimes, evaluation stacks, vector databases, and developer workflow tooling for production LLM systems.
